1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,325
Welcome to Session six, the session where the structure itself becomes the data.

2
00:00:05,325 --> 00:00:10,650
Let me start with the joke from the page, because it is also the plot of the third block.

3
00:00:10,650 --> 00:00:13,200
Haemoglobin walks into a bar.

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:23,050
The bartender says, "You look tense." Haemoglobin says, "Give me some oxygen and I'll relax." That is a real description of a real mechanism, and we come back to it.

5
00:00:23,050 --> 00:00:34,150
The question for the session is this: what information about a protein is contained in its sequence, what becomes visible only in three dimensions, and how can a computer connect the two?

6
00:00:34,150 --> 00:00:40,400
Sessions three to five worked with sequences — retrieving them, aligning them, searching for families.

7
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:49,325
Structures appeared in Session three, but only as database records: a PDB entry, an AlphaFold model, an accession to write down.

8
00:00:49,325 --> 00:00:52,925
Today the coordinates themselves are what we work with.

9
00:00:52,925 --> 00:01:00,975
We turn them into distances, into secondary structure, into domains and interfaces, and we compare them with other structures.

10
00:01:00,975 --> 00:01:03,700
And the globin thread continues.

11
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:07,425
Session three found beta-globin's identifiers.

12
00:01:07,425 --> 00:01:10,725
Session four aligned globin sequences.

13
00:01:10,725 --> 00:01:14,000
Session five searched for the globin family.

14
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,325
Today we compare globin structures — and we will see them succeed exactly where Session five hit a wall.

15
00:01:20,325 --> 00:01:21,950
Three blocks.

16
00:01:21,950 --> 00:01:26,875
First, how a sequence becomes a structure, and what the levels of structure are.

17
00:01:26,875 --> 00:01:30,625
Second, comparing proteins in three dimensions.

18
00:01:30,625 --> 00:01:38,650
Third, domains, conformational states, and structure as something to predict — which is where the second half of this course begins.

19
00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:44,175
One framing before we start, because it sets up the whole second half of the course.

20
00:01:44,175 --> 00:01:46,975
Today the structure is the data.

21
00:01:46,975 --> 00:01:52,100
We have the coordinates; we are going to look at what they tell us that a sequence cannot.

22
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:58,900
From Session seven onwards the question inverts: can a computer predict these things from the sequence alone?

23
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:07,600
Every structural property we meet today — the secondary structure, the contact map, the fold, the interface — becomes a prediction target later.

24
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:15,425
So it is worth paying attention today not only to what these properties are, but to how hard each one looks to guess from sequence.

25
00:02:15,425 --> 00:02:18,375
Some of them turn out to be nearly easy.

26
00:02:18,375 --> 00:02:20,725
Others took fifty years.

27
00:02:21,325 --> 00:02:22,925
First block.

28
00:02:22,925 --> 00:02:27,575
A chain of amino acids becomes a specific three-dimensional shape.

29
00:02:27,575 --> 00:02:33,050
What are the levels of that description, and what does the chain actually have freedom to do?

30
00:02:33,650 --> 00:02:42,300
Structural biology describes a folded protein at four levels, and haemoglobin has all four at once, which is why it is the example.

31
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:45,875
Primary structure is the amino-acid sequence.

32
00:02:45,875 --> 00:02:49,725
That is what Sessions three to five worked with entirely.

33
00:02:49,725 --> 00:02:57,050
Secondary structure is local backbone shape: alpha-helices, beta-strands, and everything else, which is called coil.

34
00:02:57,050 --> 00:03:04,050
It is local in a precise sense — it is determined by interactions between residues close together in the chain.

35
00:03:04,050 --> 00:03:11,200
Tertiary structure is the three-dimensional fold of one chain: how the helices and strands pack against each other.

36
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:16,775
This requires long-range interactions, between residues far apart in the sequence.

37
00:03:16,775 --> 00:03:22,000
Quaternary structure is several folded chains packed together into an assembly.

38
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,475
Haemoglobin shows you all four simultaneously.

39
00:03:25,475 --> 00:03:34,225
It has a sequence, it is largely helical, each chain folds into the globin fold, and four of those chains assemble into the working molecule.

40
00:03:34,225 --> 00:03:37,375
The levels are not competing descriptions.

41
00:03:37,375 --> 00:03:41,775
They are a hierarchy, and each one is built out of the one below it.

42
00:03:42,375 --> 00:03:48,200
And each level brings its own computational problem, which is how this course is organised.

43
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:51,950
Primary structure: alignment, BLAST, profiles.

44
00:03:51,950 --> 00:03:54,525
That was Sessions three to five.

45
00:03:54,525 --> 00:03:58,075
Secondary structure: prediction from sequence.

46
00:03:58,075 --> 00:04:05,075
That is Sessions seven to eleven — most of the machine-learning half of the course is aimed at this and its relatives.

47
00:04:05,075 --> 00:04:09,075
Tertiary structure: comparing structures and predicting them.

48
00:04:09,075 --> 00:04:12,800
Comparison is today; prediction is Session twelve.

49
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:16,800
Quaternary structure: interfaces and complexes.

50
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:20,375
Again, partly today and fully in Session twelve.

51
00:04:20,375 --> 00:04:24,575
So the course follows the levels in order, and we are at the hinge.

52
00:04:24,575 --> 00:04:27,725
Everything before today was sequences.

53
00:04:27,725 --> 00:04:30,750
Everything after today is prediction.

54
00:04:30,750 --> 00:04:35,175
Today is the one session where we simply look at structures as they are.

55
00:04:35,775 --> 00:04:45,300
A protein chain is called flexible, and that is true, but it is far narrower than it sounds — and this narrowness is what makes structure prediction possible at all.

56
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:49,650
Each residue contributes three dihedral angles to the backbone.

57
00:04:49,650 --> 00:04:52,875
Phi turns about the N-C-alpha bond.

58
00:04:52,875 --> 00:04:55,450
Psi turns about C-alpha-C.

59
00:04:55,450 --> 00:05:00,350
And omega turns about the peptide bond joining one residue to the next.

60
00:05:00,350 --> 00:05:03,100
Only the first two actually turn.

61
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:12,875
The peptide bond has partial double-bond character, which means it resists rotation, so omega is held near a hundred and eighty degrees — the trans form.

62
00:05:12,875 --> 00:05:22,900
About three residues in a thousand are found in the cis form instead, usually before a proline, and those are worth noticing when you meet one, but they are exceptions.

63
00:05:22,900 --> 00:05:27,300
So a backbone conformation is two numbers per residue, not three.

64
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:30,075
That is the entire search space.

65
00:05:30,075 --> 00:05:38,025
It is also why a Ramachandran plot — phi against psi — can show the conformation of a whole chain on a single pair of axes.

66
00:05:38,025 --> 00:05:41,475
The right-hand panel is not a textbook diagram.

67
00:05:41,475 --> 00:05:43,875
It is this chain, measured.

68
00:05:43,875 --> 00:05:48,325
Every residue of beta-globin, plotted by its own two angles.

69
00:05:48,325 --> 00:05:55,800
The helix residues fall into one tight cluster, because that is what a helix is: the same pair of angles, repeated.

70
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:58,225
The coil residues scatter.

71
00:05:58,225 --> 00:06:06,575
And there are no strands at all — which agrees with the eighty point eight per cent helix and zero per cent strand we measure later in the lecture.

72
00:06:06,575 --> 00:06:09,550
Nobody drew those clusters on the plot.

73
00:06:09,550 --> 00:06:12,625
That is simply where the residues landed.

74
00:06:13,225 --> 00:06:23,125
The three classes of secondary structure, defined by hydrogen bonds between backbone groups — the N-H and the C-equals-O of the backbone, not the side chains.

75
00:06:23,125 --> 00:06:29,900
An alpha-helix is a coil held together by hydrogen bonds from residue i to residue i plus four.

76
00:06:29,900 --> 00:06:38,775
That spacing is what produces the pitch of the helix, and it is strictly local: everything holding a helix together lies within four residues.

77
00:06:38,775 --> 00:06:45,600
A beta-strand is an extended stretch, hydrogen-bonded edge to edge with other strands to form a sheet.

78
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:49,400
Note the difference — a strand is not stable by itself.

79
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:54,025
It needs its partners, and those partners can be anywhere in the sequence.

80
00:06:54,025 --> 00:06:59,625
So a sheet is partly a long-range structure even though we call the strands secondary.

81
00:06:59,625 --> 00:07:01,925
Everything else is coil.

82
00:07:01,925 --> 00:07:09,925
Not disorder, and not an absence of structure: coil is a perfectly definite shape, it is just not one of the two repeating ones.

83
00:07:09,925 --> 00:07:14,575
The protein shown is ubiquitin, because it has all three classes.

84
00:07:14,575 --> 00:07:19,325
Haemoglobin has no strands at all, so it would be a poor illustration here.

85
00:07:19,325 --> 00:07:22,675
And these labels are assigned, not observed.

86
00:07:22,675 --> 00:07:33,075
Programs like DSSP, or PyMOL's dss, take the coordinates of a solved structure and apply hydrogen-bonding rules to decide which residue gets which label.

87
00:07:33,075 --> 00:07:41,825
That matters later, because when we train a model to predict secondary structure, these program-assigned labels are the ground truth we train against.

88
00:07:42,425 --> 00:07:45,625
What actually holds a tertiary fold together?

89
00:07:45,625 --> 00:07:48,875
Mainly one thing: the hydrophobic effect.

90
00:07:48,875 --> 00:07:58,825
Non-polar side chains are pushed away from water, so they end up buried in the interior of the protein, while polar and charged ones sit on the surface where water is.

91
00:07:58,825 --> 00:08:05,650
The fold is to a large extent what you get when a chain arranges itself so the oily residues are inside.

92
00:08:05,650 --> 00:08:09,800
This is why Session four's sequence logos showed what they showed.

93
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:20,150
Those columns that were not conserved as a residue but were always hydrophobic are buried positions — the constraint is "must be oily", and several residues satisfy it.

94
00:08:20,150 --> 00:08:22,775
Other interactions contribute.

95
00:08:22,775 --> 00:08:27,325
Hydrogen bonds, electrostatics, van der Waals contacts.

96
00:08:27,325 --> 00:08:30,000
Two are worth naming specifically.

97
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:39,550
A salt bridge is an electrostatic interaction between oppositely charged side chains — a lysine or arginine against an aspartate or glutamate.

98
00:08:39,550 --> 00:08:51,200
And a disulfide bond is a covalent link between two cysteine sulphurs, far stronger than any of the others, and the only one of these that is a real chemical bond rather than an attraction.

99
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:53,125
Finally, scale.

100
00:08:53,125 --> 00:08:56,225
Many proteins work as a single chain.

101
00:08:56,225 --> 00:09:00,950
Others assemble into complexes of anything from two chains to hundreds.

102
00:09:01,550 --> 00:09:03,975
Haemoglobin specifically.

103
00:09:03,975 --> 00:09:12,700
It is a heterotetramer: two alpha-globin chains of a hundred and forty-one residues, and two beta-globin chains of a hundred and forty-six.

104
00:09:12,700 --> 00:09:17,925
Beta-globin is HBB, the protein this course has followed since Session three.

105
00:09:17,925 --> 00:09:23,925
Each chain folds independently and carries one haem group, which is what binds the oxygen.

106
00:09:23,925 --> 00:09:29,025
In the figure the alpha chains are blue and cyan, the beta chains salmon and pink.

107
00:09:29,025 --> 00:09:35,425
Now a measurement, because this is the kind of thing you should get from the coordinates rather than from a textbook.

108
00:09:35,425 --> 00:09:47,250
Using PyMOL's secondary-structure algorithm, one beta chain is a hundred and eighteen of a hundred and forty-six residues helical — eighty point eight per cent — and zero per cent strand.

109
00:09:47,250 --> 00:09:50,450
A protein essentially made of helices.

110
00:09:50,450 --> 00:09:54,875
One detail worth flagging, because it will matter in Session seven.

111
00:09:54,875 --> 00:10:02,425
The PDB entry's own annotation gives a hundred and seventeen helical residues, not a hundred and eighteen.

112
00:10:02,425 --> 00:10:06,325
The methods disagree about where exactly a helix ends.

113
00:10:06,325 --> 00:10:12,750
That is a difference of one residue out of a hundred and forty-six, so it changes nothing biologically.

114
00:10:12,750 --> 00:10:21,875
But if you are training a model to predict secondary structure, that one residue is a label, and your ground truth now depends on which program assigned it.

115
00:10:21,875 --> 00:10:26,975
Keep that in mind when we start quoting prediction accuracies to the decimal point.

116
00:10:27,575 --> 00:10:34,750
Let this run for a moment and watch it, because a still picture of a protein hides the one thing a structure is actually for.

117
00:10:34,750 --> 00:10:37,050
A still is a projection.

118
00:10:37,050 --> 00:10:45,575
It flattens three dimensions into two and throws away what is near what — which is precisely the information a structure exists to give you.

119
00:10:45,575 --> 00:10:47,875
Turning it brings that back.

120
00:10:47,875 --> 00:11:01,125
Watch the two kinds of chain packing against each other: the alpha chains in blue, the beta chains in tan, meeting at interfaces that are a real part of the machine rather than an accident of how four chains were put in a box.

121
00:11:01,125 --> 00:11:05,250
Watch the central cavity open and close as it goes round.

122
00:11:05,250 --> 00:11:09,925
It is there in every still too, but you have to be told; here you see it.

123
00:11:09,925 --> 00:11:14,350
And watch the four haem groups, the red spheres, pass in and out of view.

124
00:11:14,350 --> 00:11:16,450
Notice where they are.

125
00:11:16,450 --> 00:11:22,425
Each one is buried inside its own subunit, not at the interfaces between subunits.

126
00:11:22,425 --> 00:11:33,075
Which raises the question the third block answers: if the oxygen binds inside each subunit separately, how does binding at one subunit make binding at the next one easier?

127
00:11:33,075 --> 00:11:36,300
One revolution, ten degrees at a time.

128
00:11:36,300 --> 00:11:39,525
Nobody has to open PyMOL to see this.

129
00:11:40,125 --> 00:11:44,400
Now the question underneath the entire second half of this course.

130
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:47,350
Does the sequence determine the structure?

131
00:11:47,350 --> 00:11:53,450
Because if it does not, predicting structure from sequence is not merely hard, it is impossible.

132
00:11:53,450 --> 00:11:58,250
The classic evidence is Christian Anfinsen's, from the nineteen sixties.

133
00:11:58,250 --> 00:12:05,175
He took ribonuclease, unfolded it in a test tube with a denaturant, and then removed the denaturant.

134
00:12:05,175 --> 00:12:14,200
The protein refolded into its active shape on its own — no cellular machinery, no instructions, nothing present but the chain and the solvent.

135
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:22,625
So for many small globular proteins, the sequence contains enough information to specify the native fold under the right conditions.

136
00:12:22,625 --> 00:12:27,075
That principle is what makes structure prediction a sensible goal.

137
00:12:27,075 --> 00:12:31,450
But it is not a law for every protein, and the exceptions matter.

138
00:12:31,450 --> 00:12:41,150
Some proteins need chaperones to fold efficiently — the information may be in the sequence, but the chain cannot find it unaided at cellular concentrations.

139
00:12:41,150 --> 00:12:47,875
Many need a ligand, a cofactor or a binding partner, and without it there is no stable fold to find.

140
00:12:47,875 --> 00:12:53,500
Some switch between alternative states — haemoglobin itself, as the third block shows.

141
00:12:53,500 --> 00:12:59,925
And intrinsically disordered regions have no single fold at all; for them the question is malformed.

142
00:12:59,925 --> 00:13:07,625
Worth holding onto when you see a prediction server return exactly one confident structure for any sequence you give it.

143
00:13:08,225 --> 00:13:16,325
Two kinds of interaction build a fold, and the difference between them explains the entire difficulty gradient of structure prediction.

144
00:13:16,325 --> 00:13:22,575
Local interactions join residues close together in the sequence, and they make secondary structure.

145
00:13:22,575 --> 00:13:31,025
An alpha-helix's hydrogen bonds connect residue i to residue i plus four — you never need to look further than four residues away.

146
00:13:31,025 --> 00:13:40,100
Long-range interactions join residues far apart in the sequence but close in space, and they pack the helices and strands into a tertiary fold.

147
00:13:40,100 --> 00:13:45,200
Two residues a hundred positions apart can be neighbours in three dimensions.

148
00:13:45,200 --> 00:13:47,250
Now the consequence.

149
00:13:47,250 --> 00:13:55,250
Local structure can partly be guessed from a short window of sequence, because the information determining it is inside that window.

150
00:13:55,250 --> 00:14:03,200
That is Session seven, and it is why a method from nineteen ninety-three using a window of thirteen residues gets anywhere at all.

151
00:14:03,200 --> 00:14:09,150
Long-range structure cannot be guessed from a window, because no window contains both partners.

152
00:14:09,150 --> 00:14:19,325
To know that residue twenty touches residue one hundred and twenty you need information about the pair, and a sliding window over the sequence structurally cannot provide it.

153
00:14:19,325 --> 00:14:28,975
That is why secondary-structure prediction was partly solved in the nineteen nineties and tertiary-structure prediction was not solved until twenty twenty.

154
00:14:28,975 --> 00:14:32,150
The two problems look adjacent and are not.

155
00:14:32,750 --> 00:14:43,050
A structure file is a list of atomic coordinates, but coordinates are often not the most useful representation, and here is an alternative that matters enormously later.

156
00:14:43,050 --> 00:14:46,525
Take the distance between every pair of residues.

157
00:14:46,525 --> 00:14:50,975
For a chain of L residues that gives an L by L distance map.

158
00:14:50,975 --> 00:14:59,975
Threshold it — here, C-beta atoms within eight ångström — and you get a contact map: one where two residues are close, zero elsewhere.

159
00:14:59,975 --> 00:15:03,525
The property that makes these useful is invariance.

160
00:15:03,525 --> 00:15:10,175
Coordinates change when you rotate or translate a protein; the distances between its residues do not.

161
00:15:10,175 --> 00:15:17,975
So two structures can be compared as maps without first superimposing them, which removes a whole step and a whole class of problem.

162
00:15:17,975 --> 00:15:20,350
Read the map on the slide.

163
00:15:20,350 --> 00:15:28,350
The band along the diagonal is each helix in contact with itself — local interactions, residues near each other in sequence.

164
00:15:28,350 --> 00:15:37,375
The stripes away from the diagonal are helices packed against one another — long-range interactions, exactly the ones a sequence window cannot see.

165
00:15:37,375 --> 00:15:42,025
The eight red bars are the eight alpha-helices from the PDB file.

166
00:15:42,025 --> 00:15:55,725
The notebook counts a hundred and ninety-seven contacts between residues at least six apart in sequence, which is the number that matters: contacts between near-neighbours in sequence are trivially there, and the interesting ones are the rest.

167
00:15:55,725 --> 00:15:58,750
This representation returns twice.

168
00:15:58,750 --> 00:16:06,825
In Session ten, because an L by L map looks like an image, and image-processing networks turn out to be good at predicting it.

169
00:16:06,825 --> 00:16:16,725
And in Session twelve, where coevolution, contact prediction and AlphaFold's pair representation all work on residue-pair information shaped exactly like this.

170
00:16:17,325 --> 00:16:22,825
Step back for a second, because this is one of the ideas the course keeps returning to.

171
00:16:22,825 --> 00:16:25,650
The same protein can be a sequence.

172
00:16:25,650 --> 00:16:28,425
Or a profile, from Session five.

173
00:16:28,425 --> 00:16:30,725
Or a set of coordinates.

174
00:16:30,725 --> 00:16:33,825
Or a distance map, or a contact map.

175
00:16:33,825 --> 00:16:37,200
Or — from Session eleven — a learned embedding.

176
00:16:37,200 --> 00:16:41,625
None of these is the protein, and none is more correct than the others.

177
00:16:41,625 --> 00:16:46,125
They are descriptions, and each one makes different patterns easy to see.

178
00:16:46,125 --> 00:16:49,375
A sequence makes it easy to see a motif.

179
00:16:49,375 --> 00:16:55,775
A contact map makes it easy to see which helices pack together, which the sequence hides completely.

180
00:16:55,775 --> 00:17:02,150
And later in the course the choice does more than that: it decides which patterns a model can easily learn.

181
00:17:02,150 --> 00:17:08,600
A network given coordinates has to work out for itself that rotating a protein does not change it.

182
00:17:08,600 --> 00:17:12,175
A network given a distance map gets that for free.

183
00:17:12,175 --> 00:17:22,250
Much of the progress in this field has come from changing the representation rather than from changing the model, and Foldseek, later this session, is a clean example.

184
00:17:22,850 --> 00:17:25,600
To look at a structure you need a viewer.

185
00:17:25,600 --> 00:17:31,150
PyMOL and ChimeraX are the two most widely used, and open-source PyMOL is free.

186
00:17:31,150 --> 00:17:36,075
Three representations of the same chain, each answering a different question.

187
00:17:36,075 --> 00:17:40,425
Cartoon draws the secondary structure as ribbons and arrows.

188
00:17:40,425 --> 00:17:45,700
Use it to see the architecture — how many helices, how they lie against each other.

189
00:17:45,700 --> 00:17:49,525
It is an abstraction: there are no ribbons in a protein.

190
00:17:49,525 --> 00:17:52,050
Sticks draws every bond.

191
00:17:52,050 --> 00:17:59,050
Use it when you need an actual side chain or a ligand, where the cartoon has abstracted away the thing you care about.

192
00:17:59,050 --> 00:18:03,125
Spheres draws every atom at roughly its real radius.

193
00:18:03,125 --> 00:18:12,025
Use it for packing — this is the one that shows you a protein is a dense object with no room inside, which the cartoon makes very easy to forget.

194
00:18:12,025 --> 00:18:14,425
PyMOL also measures.

195
00:18:14,425 --> 00:18:18,475
Distances, angles, contacts, straight from the coordinates.

196
00:18:18,475 --> 00:18:25,100
And that is the more important half of what it does, because measuring is checkable in a way that looking is not.

197
00:18:25,700 --> 00:18:32,175
Here is the alpha-helix's hydrogen bonding, and it is worth saying exactly how this figure was made.

198
00:18:32,175 --> 00:18:43,150
The helix is held by N-H to O-equals-C bonds from residue i to residue i plus four — the pattern from earlier in the block, now shown on a real helix from a real structure.

199
00:18:43,150 --> 00:18:45,650
Nobody placed these bonds.

200
00:18:45,650 --> 00:18:49,050
Nobody decided where they ought to be and drew them.

201
00:18:49,050 --> 00:18:56,400
PyMOL applied its polar-contact rules to the deposited coordinates, with a distance cutoff, and drew what it found.

202
00:18:56,400 --> 00:18:59,475
That distinction is the point of the slide.

203
00:18:59,475 --> 00:19:06,475
If the cutoff is wrong, or the wrong residues were selected, the fix is one line changed and the script rerun.

204
00:19:06,475 --> 00:19:13,925
The figure can be wrong, and if it is wrong it can be corrected, and you can see from the script what would have to be true for it to be right.

205
00:19:13,925 --> 00:19:18,025
A figure drawn by hand cannot be wrong in that useful way.

206
00:19:18,025 --> 00:19:20,625
It can only be unconvincing.

207
00:19:21,225 --> 00:19:23,500
And here is the script.

208
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:25,200
Eight lines.

209
00:19:25,200 --> 00:19:27,825
Fetch the structure, two-H-H-B.

210
00:19:27,825 --> 00:19:34,400
Create a selection containing chain B, residues a hundred and twenty-five to a hundred and forty-four.

211
00:19:34,400 --> 00:19:36,675
Show it as a cartoon.

212
00:19:36,675 --> 00:19:39,425
Show the backbone atoms as sticks.

213
00:19:39,425 --> 00:19:48,350
Then the measurement: distances from backbone nitrogens to backbone oxygens within three point five ångström, in the mode that labels them.

214
00:19:48,350 --> 00:19:50,625
Colour the dashes yellow.

215
00:19:50,625 --> 00:19:58,050
I want to labour this for a minute, because it is a habit rather than a tool, and it is the habit this course is trying to instil.

216
00:19:58,050 --> 00:20:05,150
A figure drawn by hand — ovals for atoms, extruded cylinders for bonds — cannot be checked against anything.

217
00:20:05,150 --> 00:20:10,125
There is no record of which structure it came from, which residues, which cutoff.

218
00:20:10,125 --> 00:20:16,000
And correcting it means moving shapes one at a time, so in practice it does not get corrected.

219
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:19,675
A figure made by a script carries its own provenance.

220
00:20:19,675 --> 00:20:22,275
The PDB entry is in it.

221
00:20:22,275 --> 00:20:24,750
The residue range is in it.

222
00:20:24,750 --> 00:20:28,575
The three point five ångström cutoff is in it.

223
00:20:28,575 --> 00:20:35,400
A reviewer who thinks the cutoff is too generous can say so, specifically, and you can rerun it in seconds.

224
00:20:35,400 --> 00:20:38,600
From the lab onwards, make your figures this way.

